WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.056 --> 00:00:17.198
When Oliver came down to the housekeeper's room one morning, he glanced up at the portrait of the

2
00:00:17.238 --> 00:00:19.739
lady. But it wasn't there anymore.

3
00:00:19.759 --> 00:00:22.100
"Why have they taken it away?"

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.080
he asked Mrs.

5
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.921
Bedwin. "Mr.

6
00:00:24.961 --> 00:00:26.281
Brownlow thought it bothered you.

7
00:00:27.342 --> 00:00:29.583
He didn't want it to prevent you from getting well."

8
00:00:29.603 --> 00:00:32.264
"Oh, no, it didn't bother me.

9
00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:34.076
I liked to see it.

10
00:00:35.097 --> 00:00:36.017
I quite loved it."

11
00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:41.120
"Well, you get better as fast as you can, dear, and it will be hung up again.

12
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.681
Now let's talk of something else."

13
00:00:44.781 --> 00:00:46.222
Those were happy days for Oliver.

14
00:00:47.342 --> 00:00:49.623
Everything was so quiet and neat in this house.

15
00:00:50.844 --> 00:00:52.424
Everybody was so kind and gentle.

16
00:00:53.485 --> 00:00:56.546
Mr. Brownlow bought Oliver a new set of clothes and shoes.

17
00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:00.348
Oliver gave his old things to a servant who had been nice to him.

18
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:04.193
He wanted her to sell the clothes and keep the money for herself.

19
00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:07.234
Late one afternoon Mr.

20
00:01:07.254 --> 00:01:09.594
Brownlow asked Oliver to come upstairs to his study.

21
00:01:10.755 --> 00:01:14.576
Oliver found himself in a little room full of books overlooking a pleasant garden.

22
00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:17.337
Mr. Brownlow was reading at a table.

23
00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:21.278
"Now I want you to pay attention to what I'm saying," began Mr.

24
00:01:21.318 --> 00:01:24.379
Brownlow. Oliver instantly felt alarmed.

25
00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:27.860
"Oh, don't tell me you're going to send me away!"

26
00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:32.145
"My dear child, I will never abandon you unless you give me cause."

27
00:01:33.305 --> 00:01:34.266
"I never will, sir."

28
00:01:34.286 --> 00:01:35.966
"I hope not.

29
00:01:36.947 --> 00:01:39.567
I've been deceived in the past by people whom I've tried to help.

30
00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:42.268
But I feel strongly that I can trust you.

31
00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:47.070
I only want you to know that I've suffered great pain and sorrow so that you'll be careful

32
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:47.870
not to wound me.

33
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.251
Now I want to hear your story.

34
00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:52.211
Who were your parents?

35
00:01:53.452 --> 00:01:54.272
Where did you live?"

36
00:01:55.384 --> 00:01:57.425
But before Oliver could respond, Mrs.

37
00:01:57.485 --> 00:01:59.626
Bedwin came in and laid some books on the table.

38
00:02:01.307 --> 00:02:03.088
The bookseller's boy had just delivered them.

39
00:02:03.108 --> 00:02:05.930
"Ask the boy to wait," said Mr.

40
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.392
Brownlow. "He has gone, sir," replied Mrs.

41
00:02:09.432 --> 00:02:12.053
Bedwin. "Call down the street after him.

42
00:02:13.054 --> 00:02:15.515
The bookseller is a poor man, and these books are not paid for.

43
00:02:16.716 --> 00:02:18.237
I want him to have the money today.

44
00:02:19.297 --> 00:02:20.978
There are some books to be taken back too."

45
00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:24.120
Mrs. Bedwin and Oliver rushed downstairs.

46
00:02:25.292 --> 00:02:27.714
The housekeeper stood on the front step and called for the boy.

47
00:02:28.875 --> 00:02:31.757
Oliver ran one way down the street and a servant girl ran the other.

48
00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:36.221
Soon Oliver and the girl returned to report that they couldn't find the boy.

49
00:02:36.241 --> 00:02:39.904
"Dear me, I'm very sorry about that," said Mr.

50
00:02:39.924 --> 00:02:41.045
Brownlow, who had joined them.

51
00:02:41.065 --> 00:02:44.548
"I particularly wished those books to be returned tonight."

52
00:02:44.568 --> 00:02:49.672
"Send Oliver to the bookseller's with them," suggested Mrs.

53
00:02:49.712 --> 00:02:53.936
Bedwin. "Yes, do let me take them, sir," said Oliver.

54
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.961
"I'll run all the way."

55
00:02:57.021 --> 00:02:58.162
The old gentleman nodded.

56
00:02:58.182 --> 00:03:00.784
"The books are on a chair by my table.

57
00:03:01.865 --> 00:03:02.565
Please fetch them."

58
00:03:03.606 --> 00:03:06.948
Delighted to be of use, Oliver brought down the books in a great bustle.

59
00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:10.891
He waited, cap in hand, to hear what message he was to take.

60
00:03:10.911 --> 00:03:15.454
"You're to say that you've brought those books back because I've decided not to buy them.

61
00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:18.276
You should then pay the four pounds I owe."

62
00:03:19.357 --> 00:03:21.058
Mr. Brownlow handed Oliver some money.

63
00:03:21.078 --> 00:03:25.185
"This is a five-pound note, so you must bring back the change."

64
00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:27.707
"I'll be back in ten minutes, sir."

65
00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:31.250
Oliver put the money in his pocket and picked up the books.

66
00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.452
Then he made a respectful bow and left the room.

67
00:03:36.273 --> 00:03:38.095
Mrs. Bedwin followed him to the front door.

68
00:03:39.155 --> 00:03:40.596
"Bless his sweet face!"

69
00:03:40.997 --> 00:03:42.318
she said as Oliver departed.

70
00:03:42.338 --> 00:03:45.980
"Somehow I can't bear to let him out of my sight."

71
00:03:47.121 --> 00:03:50.704
At that moment Oliver looked back at her and nodded before he turned the corner.

72
00:03:51.808 --> 00:03:54.150
Mrs. Bedwin smiled back at him and closed the door.

73
00:03:55.311 --> 00:03:57.612
Mr. Brownlow had returned to his study upstairs.

74
00:03:57.632 --> 00:04:03.337
"Let me see, he'll be back in twenty minutes at the most," he murmured, pulling out his watch

75
00:04:03.437 --> 00:04:04.437
and laying it on the table.

76
00:04:05.558 --> 00:04:06.699
"It'll be dark by that time."

77
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.981
Meanwhile Oliver walked toward the bookseller's.

78
00:04:11.142 --> 00:04:14.645
When he was almost there, he accidentally turned down a side street.

79
00:04:15.746 --> 00:04:18.688
He didn't realize his mistake until he was halfway down the street.

80
00:04:19.776 --> 00:04:22.858
Thinking it must lead in the right direction, he didn't turn back.

81
00:04:24.719 --> 00:04:29.001
He was walking along, thinking how happy he was, when he was startled by a young woman.

82
00:04:29.021 --> 00:04:31.602
"Oh, my dear brother!"

83
00:04:31.963 --> 00:04:34.464
she screamed, throwing her arms tightly around his neck.

84
00:04:35.604 --> 00:04:37.485
"Don't!" cried Oliver, struggling.

85
00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:38.026
"Let go of me.

86
00:04:38.046 --> 00:04:38.286
Who are you?

87
00:04:38.306 --> 00:04:38.826
Why are you stopping me?"

88
00:04:38.846 --> 00:04:40.867
The only reply to this was more cries from the young woman, who was wearing a white apron

89
00:04:40.887 --> 00:04:41.668
and carrying a little basket.

90
00:04:41.688 --> 00:04:42.228
"Oh, my gracious!"

91
00:04:42.268 --> 00:04:42.748
said the young woman.

92
00:04:42.788 --> 00:04:43.108
"I've found him!

93
00:04:43.128 --> 00:04:43.349
Oh, Oliver!

94
00:04:43.369 --> 00:04:44.849
Oh, you naughty boy to make me suffer such distress on your account."

95
00:04:44.869 --> 00:04:49.132
And with that she burst into such tears that a woman passing by asked if she needed a

96
00:04:49.172 --> 00:04:52.173
doctor. "Oh, no, never mind.

97
00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:53.934
I'm better now."

98
00:04:54.935 --> 00:04:56.696
The young woman grasped Oliver's hand.

99
00:04:56.716 --> 00:04:59.658
"Come home right now, you cruel boy."

100
00:04:59.678 --> 00:05:02.800
"What's the matter, ma'am?"

101
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.201
inquired the other woman.

102
00:05:04.221 --> 00:05:09.925
"About a month ago he ran away from his parents, who are hardworking and respectable people.

103
00:05:11.046 --> 00:05:14.088
He went and joined a group of thieves and almost broke his mother's heart."

104
00:05:14.108 --> 00:05:18.070
"Do go home, you little brute," said the other woman.

105
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.077
"But I don't know her," said Oliver, greatly alarmed.

106
00:05:22.097 --> 00:05:25.618
"I don't have a sister or a mother or father.

107
00:05:26.778 --> 00:05:28.259
I'm an orphan and I live with Mr.

108
00:05:28.299 --> 00:05:29.079
Brownlow now."

109
00:05:29.099 --> 00:05:33.340
"Oh, hear, how he keeps it up," said the young woman.

110
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.041
"Why, it's Nancy!"

111
00:05:36.441 --> 00:05:39.502
exclaimed Oliver, who now saw her face for the first time.

112
00:05:39.522 --> 00:05:42.163
"You see, he does know me!"

113
00:05:42.563 --> 00:05:45.064
cried Nancy, appealing to the crowd that had begun to gather.

114
00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.821
"Make him come home before he kills his dear mother and father and breaks my heart."

115
00:05:50.841 --> 00:05:53.362
"What on earth is this?"

116
00:05:53.822 --> 00:05:56.843
said a man, bursting out from a pub with a white dog at his heels.

117
00:05:58.003 --> 00:05:59.724
"Young Oliver, come home directly."

118
00:05:59.744 --> 00:06:01.884
"I don't belong to them.

119
00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:03.785
I don't know them.

120
00:06:04.125 --> 00:06:05.165
Help! Help!"

121
00:06:06.346 --> 00:06:08.486
Oliver struggled in the man's powerful grasp.

122
00:06:09.686 --> 00:06:12.787
"Yes, I'll help you, you young rascal," said the man.

123
00:06:12.807 --> 00:06:14.808
"Whose books are these?

124
00:06:16.032 --> 00:06:17.613
You've been stealing them, haven't you?"

125
00:06:18.694 --> 00:06:22.476
With that, he snatched the books from Oliver's grasp and dragged the boy away.

126
00:06:23.677 --> 00:06:24.077
At Mr.

127
00:06:24.097 --> 00:06:26.099
Brownlow's house, the gas lamps were lighted.

128
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.781
Mrs. Bedwin was waiting anxiously at the open door.

129
00:06:30.902 --> 00:06:34.765
A servant had run up the street many times to see if there was any trace of Oliver.

130
00:06:35.905 --> 00:06:36.286
And Mr.

131
00:06:36.306 --> 00:06:39.688
Brownlow sat in his dark study with his watch on the table.
